{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons","id":1100228,"title":"Other African pantheons?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-04T15:11:08-07:00","id":1100228,"title":"Other African pantheons?","content":"I'd like to see more African pantheons besides just the Orishas and the Netjer, but I'm not sure which ones. The Egyptian and Yoruba gods are by far the easiest to research, but what other cultural groups do you think would work? They would have to be pantheons with a fair number of suitable beings, with information about them available. The Bantu and Igbo pantheons seem the most promising to me, but information on them is still fairly scarce. What others do you think would work?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T15:33:54-07:00","id":1100231,"title":"","content":"The Ashanti Pantheon, the Abosom, are a really interesting West-African Pantheon. The world at large probably knows about them through Anansi, but probably don't know much about them beyond the story telling Spider. Myself and Sacerdos have actually worked at writing a Pantheon for themselves which we find entirely viable. We have done research on them, done some heavy outlining, and got some rough drafts hammered out to represent them as a Pantheon actually, and it appears that it will be entirely viable. We are going to wait for Scion Second Edition to come out so we can give them to the community in the new and shiny system, might take a bit to make the mechanics though. Houseruling Pantheons for 1e's main slowdown was doing all of the mechanics, PsPs are the bane of my existance. They are not in any presentable state at the moment however, my writing normally needs a massive amount of editing since I have some terrible writing habits that Iry fixes for me.<br /> <br /> They are semi-Monothesitic, with only one entity regarded as a 'Deity,' with a host of subordinate entities who draw power through that main Monotheistic entity named Nyame. Interestingly, Anansi isn't even technically even one of these subservient entities, and is not worshiped either. He can be stated out as a Divine Parent though, I'm sure, it's just an interesting little detail that he isn't actually one of the Abosom, just an enigma of an entity who hangs around in myth and folklore.<br /> <br /> They were born out of an idea Sacerdos and I came up with of making 'mini Pantheons,' (Mini only in that they have fewer Deities, they will still probably be pretty long) to better represent cultures that have very small, or very localized Pantheons. They will probably only have four-ish Major Deities, but a whole host of information about them otherwise. They've given us a really great experience dealing with the borderline-Monotheistic cultures, which I hope give us the tools to include other Pantheons who would normally be overlooked.<br /> <br /> If you want to know anything about the Abosom Wannabe, I could talk about them for sure.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":".  I'm bad at words 06-04-2017, 06:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T16:08:42-07:00","id":1100247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100231#post1100231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Ashanti Pantheon, the Abosom, are a really interesting West-African Pantheon. The world at large probably knows about them through Anansi, but probably don't know much about them beyond the story telling Spider. Myself and Sacerdos have actually worked at writing a Pantheon for themselves which we find entirely viable. We have done research on them, done some heavy outlining, and got some rough drafts hammered out to represent them as a Pantheon actually, and it appears that it will be entirely viable. We are going to wait for Scion Second Edition to come out so we can give them to the community in the new and shiny system, might take a bit to make the mechanics though. Houseruling Pantheons for 1e's main slowdown was doing all of the mechanics, PsPs are the bane of my existance. They are not in any presentable state at the moment however, my writing normally needs a massive amount of editing since I have some terrible writing habits that Iry fixes for me.<br /> <br /> They are semi-Monothesitic, with only one entity regarded as a 'Deity,' with a host of subordinate entities who draw power through that main Monotheistic entity named Nyame. Interestingly, Anansi isn't even technically even one of these subservient entities, and is not worshiped either. He can be stated out as a Divine Parent though, I'm sure, it's just an interesting little detail that he isn't actually one of the Abosom, just an enigma of an entity who hangs around in myth and folklore.<br /> <br /> They were born out of an idea Sacerdos and I came up with of making 'mini Pantheons,' (Mini only in that they have fewer Deities, they will still probably be pretty long) to better represent cultures that have very small, or very localized Pantheons. They will probably only have four-ish Major Deities, but a whole host of information about them otherwise. They've given us a really great experience dealing with the borderline-Monotheistic cultures, which I hope give us the tools to include other Pantheons who would normally be overlooked.<br /> <br /> If you want to know anything about the Abosom Wannabe, I could talk about them for sure.</div> </div> </div> </div>What concept would you pick for their PSP, I's there any particular magic theyn perform a lot?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T17:21:10-07:00","id":1100271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100247#post1100247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What concept would you pick for their PSP, I's there any particular magic theyn perform a lot?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, they have a few systems that could be viable for a PsP for the Abosom. They do have Divine Possession, but not in any way that makes it stand out. A lot of Pantheons have Divine Possession, it's very commonplace, the Orisha, the Abosom, the Kami, and the Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s (Haida) all have it in their cosmologies. I would probably only want to represent as a PsP if it was particularly core to their identity, which for the Abosom it isn't entirely.<br /> <br /> A relatively unique, and <i>really</i> important cosmological idea they do have though is Abosom residing within man-made objects for various reasons. (They can also reside in natural objects like rocks, trees, and animals, but the more accessible ones live in man-made objects) There are Suman which are smaller objects which have very minor, almost non-sentient Divine Entities inside of them which provide various benefits to their owner as long as their taboos are kept. Then there are larger shrines which the more minor Divine Entities reside within, such as a Divinity which looks after a specific village, which have personalities and interact with people through mediums. There are lots of variation of this sort of thing through the Ashanti religious system, enough so that I think a PsP could be made from it. I am batting around the idea that someone playing an Ashanti Scion also plays the minor Abosom in their shrine they have to carry around with them.<br /> <br /> This also ties into the idea of the Golden Stool, a very important Political-Religious concept and object for the Ashanti. Stools themselves have this whole host of complex details about them, but The Golden Stool specifically is endowed with the... it's not entirely clear, but almost the Abosom of the very Ashanti State itself. And when a new King is crowned, they take the soul of the Ashanti State into themselves by quickly being seated on it for a brief moment. Wars have been fought over this stool, it's very, very important. There's a slightly similar idea with the Black Stools, sort of like anchors for ancestor spirits who hang around after achieving their goal in existence and thus ceasing reincarnation.<br /> <br /> I am not totally sure though, maybe something else will come up, or something else will be more easily represented with the new mechanics. We will see!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 06-04-2017, 08:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T18:18:51-07:00","id":1100283,"title":"","content":"The Abosom sound like a great option. Inconveniently, modern Ghanaians I've met refer to the Devil as &quot;Abonsam&quot; \u2026<br /> <br /> I'd love to do an Igbo Pantheon as part of the Afro-Atlantic confederation. They figure prominently in Puerto Rican Palo (which might also be their Pantheon's name) alongside the Orich\u00e1s, and less prominently in Haitian Vodou via Gran Ibo and his nasyon. I've bugged my professors for info on them, but all the best sources on them are in Spanish.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><a href=\"https://jamesmendezhodes.com\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Mendez</span></a></span></div> <span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><b>Currently Developing</b> you know I should probably stop updating this field, I keep accidentally announcing things early<br /> <b>Crowdfund Me </b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/mndz\" target=\"_blank\">via Patreon</a>: essays about race, religion, and culture in gaming</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8310-lula","name":"Lula","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8310_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T18:53:33-07:00","id":1100290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lula</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100283#post1100283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Abosom sound like a great option. Inconveniently, modern Ghanaians I've met refer to the Devil as &quot;Abonsam&quot; \u2026<br /> <br /> I'd love to do an Igbo Pantheon as part of the Afro-Atlantic confederation. They figure prominently in Puerto Rican Palo (which might also be their Pantheon's name) alongside the Orich\u00e1s, and less prominently in Haitian Vodou via Gran Ibo and his nasyon. I've bugged my professors for info on them, but all the best sources on them are in Spanish.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, Lula! I actually have a question for you since you are educated on the topic of the trans-Atlantic transfer of the West African religious traditions. Do we know why the Abosom were not transferred on a scale similar to some of the other West African Pantheons? There is a folkloric presence of Anansi in Jamaica along with Owu (Brer Dead) and Osebo (Brer Tiger/Lion), but we don't see Nyame, Tano, Asase Ya or really a continuation of the religious traditions.<br /> <br /> My initial thought was that it could have something to do with the more... geographically static nature of some of the Ashanti traditions? It's hard to bring your Gods with you when they live in very specific objects you couldn't bring with you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T19:27:29-07:00","id":1100296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100290#post1100290\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh, Lula! I actually have a question for you since you are educated on the topic of the trans-Atlantic transfer of the West African religious traditions. Do we know why the Abosom were not transferred on a scale similar to some of the other West African Pantheons? There is a folkloric presence of Anansi in Jamaica along with Owu (Brer Dead) and Osebo (Brer Tiger/Lion), but we don't see Nyame, Tano, Asase Ya or really a continuation of the religious traditions.<br /> <br /> My initial thought was that it could have something to do with the more... geographically static nature of some of the Ashanti traditions? It's hard to bring your Gods with you when they live in very specific objects you couldn't bring with you.</div> </div> </div> </div>I should check with Tim Burke to make sure, but I think the answer is mostly &quot;Britain.&quot; During the era of the transatlantic slave trade, England controlled the Gold Coast and Jamaica. However, abolitionist sentiment in Britain was so strong that the British Empire abolished the slave trade to its own economic detriment out of sheer moral outrage. Slavery within the British colonies persisted a while longer, but fewer Akan people wound up on slave ships.<br /> <br /> Because of where they were geographically and the colonial links, I *think* those slaves were also more likely to wind up in the United States, which systematically exterminated traces of African religion and culture much more assiduously than the Caribbean and South America. Slaves had less personal freedom in the US than in the Caribbean or South America (although you were also less likely to get your arms ripped off in a sugar mill than in the West Indies), and the dominant Protestant culture overwrote African beliefs much more aggressively than the Catholics further south. The less numerous American Catholics kept trying to catechize slaves, which was not so effective when slaves were learning English as their fifth language or whatever.<br /> <br /> This is not to say no one from the British colonies wound up in the Americas: the regions further East, the Bights of Benin and Biafra, were hit much harder and for longer, since a) there were more locals participating on a large scale in the trade (looking at you, Dahomey), and b) they were closer to the slave trade's heartland in the Kongo-Angola region. Igbos, Yor\u00f9b\u00e1s, and Dahomeans were close enough to those regions that they wound up as PoWs there and further south.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><a href=\"https://jamesmendezhodes.com\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Mendez</span></a></span></div> <span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><b>Currently Developing</b> you know I should probably stop updating this field, I keep accidentally announcing things early<br /> <b>Crowdfund Me </b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/mndz\" target=\"_blank\">via Patreon</a>: essays about race, religion, and culture in gaming</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8310-lula","name":"Lula","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8310_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T19:47:14-07:00","id":1100303,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lula</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100296#post1100296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I should check with Tim Burke to make sure, but I think the answer is mostly &quot;Britain.&quot; During the era of the transatlantic slave trade, England controlled the Gold Coast and Jamaica. However, abolitionist sentiment in Britain was so strong that the British Empire abolished the slave trade to its own economic detriment out of sheer moral outrage. Slavery within the British colonies persisted a while longer, but fewer Akan people wound up on slave ships.<br /> <br /> Because of where they were geographically and the colonial links, I *think* those slaves were also more likely to wind up in the United States, which systematically exterminated traces of African religion and culture much more assiduously than the Caribbean and South America. Slaves had less personal freedom in the US than in the Caribbean or South America (although you were also less likely to get your arms ripped off in a sugar mill than in the West Indies), and the dominant Protestant culture overwrote African beliefs much more aggressively than the Catholics further south. The less numerous American Catholics kept trying to catechize slaves, which was not so effective when slaves were learning English as their fifth language or whatever.<br /> <br /> This is not to say no one from the British colonies wound up in the Americas: the regions further East, the Bights of Benin and Biafra, were hit much harder and for longer, since a) there were more locals participating on a large scale in the trade (looking at you, Dahomey), and b) they were closer to the slave trade's heartland in the Kongo-Angola region. Igbos, Yor\u00f9b\u00e1s, and Dahomeans were close enough to those regions that they wound up as PoWs there and further south.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is absolutely fascinating, I hadn't considered the differences between Protestantism and Catholithism in that situation. Thinking about it now, I know very little of Protestant interaction with non-Abrahamic faiths, I had never really considered that they would be more evangelizing, but now that I think about it, it makes absolute sense. The colonial period areas I have had experience with are the Wyandot, Haida, and Inca, all of which were interacted with mainly with Jesuits which has probably flavored my understanding quite a bit. I should look into that, I am desperately interested in the interactions between Christianity and non-Abrahamic faiths in moments of conversion, especially looking at the compromises made during conversion efforts.<br /> <br /> Thanks so much for that Lula! That has given me quite a bit to think about, that is really interesting!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T23:12:11-07:00","id":1100347,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100303#post1100303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That is absolutely fascinating, I hadn't considered the differences between Protestantism and Catholithism in that situation. Thinking about it now, I know very little of Protestant interaction with non-Abrahamic faiths, I had never really considered that they would be more evangelizing, but now that I think about it, it makes absolute sense. The colonial period areas I have had experience with are the Wyandot, Haida, and Inca, all of which were interacted with mainly with Jesuits which has probably flavored my understanding quite a bit. I should look into that, I am desperately interested in the interactions between Christianity and non-Abrahamic faiths in moments of conversion, especially looking at the compromises made during conversion efforts.<br /> <br /> Thanks so much for that Lula! That has given me quite a bit to think about, that is really interesting!</div> </div> </div> </div>You're welcome! I could go on about the differences between Protestant American slave culture and Catholic Central and South American slave culture for weeks. Catholicism was typically permissive of syncretism and actively encouraged African folkways through systems like cabildos (African ethno-religious fraternities) as a way of impressing the religion on captives. North American Protestantism, on the other hand, laboriously scrubbed out as much African content as possible, so while specific practices and activities with African roots (ring shouts etc.) persisted, identifiably African figures and content fell away. This process was important to Frazier's thesis in the Herskovits-Frazier debate (although it wasn't nearly enough to carry it, as we now know).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><a href=\"https://jamesmendezhodes.com\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Mendez</span></a></span></div> <span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><b>Currently Developing</b> you know I should probably stop updating this field, I keep accidentally announcing things early<br /> <b>Crowdfund Me </b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/mndz\" target=\"_blank\">via Patreon</a>: essays about race, religion, and culture in gaming</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8310-lula","name":"Lula","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8310_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T23:38:08-07:00","id":1100353,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lula</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100347#post1100347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're welcome! I could go on about the differences between Protestant American slave culture and Catholic Central and South American slave culture for weeks. Catholicism was typically permissive of syncretism and actively encouraged African folkways through systems like cabildos (African ethno-religious fraternities) as a way of impressing the religion on captives. North American Protestantism, on the other hand, laboriously scrubbed out as much African content as possible, so while specific practices and activities with African roots (ring shouts etc.) persisted, identifiably African figures and content fell away. This process was important to Frazier's thesis in the Herskovits-Frazier debate (although it wasn't nearly enough to carry it, as we now know).</div> </div> </div> </div>I have never heard of the cabildos, those sound quite interesting! My propensity for ancient history reveals itself once again. Do you have any suggestions for journal articles or books to look into on that topic of permissive syncretism in this context? It's sort of become my pet topic. Sidhe being folded into popular religion's view of Christian cosmology in the early modern period in Ireland. The honestly impressively badly done Christianization of the rural Andes resulting in animal sacrifices to saints and various Christian and local Inca/Pre-Inca Divinities being folded into the same group into the modern day. I adore seeing the ways popular religion folds together these systems, and the environments which let the population 'get away with it'.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T14:33:41-07:00","id":1100591,"title":"","content":"I'd love information on the Abosom, Watcher. They sound really interesting, especially considering that I was under the impression that Anansi was the only workable figure from the Ashanti religion. I look forward to seeing your write up for them for the game as well, and I'm sure I'll end up using it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T15:24:19-07:00","id":1100618,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100591#post1100591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd love information on the Abosom, Watcher. They sound really interesting, especially considering that I was under the impression that Anansi was the only workable figure from the Ashanti religion. I look forward to seeing your write up for them for the game as well, and I'm sure I'll end up using it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can remember Gotham By Night talking about how Anansi was the only real viable figure within the Ashanti religious system, which at first was my concern when looking into their traditional religious system. When Sacerdos and I realized that there were a whole host of Deities, though admittedly most of whom are incredibly minor or localized, it suddenly appeared to be totally viable as a Pantheon. There are four figures who are undoubtedly possible Divine Parents of enough note to be considered 'Major Divinities' (I don't say Deities or Gods here since technically only one of them is a God, and only one of them is an Abosom, it's very technical) which is good enough.<br /> <br /> The first of whom, the one everyone knows, is Anansi. He serves as an object lesson in why you should be a productive member of society. Anansi is lazy, greedy, doesn't help his neighbors, and is constantly trying to challenge the will of Nyame. Anansi in most stories gets his comeuppance at the end of it. Anansi is an object lesson, &quot;Don't ignore your neighbors when they need help because they won't help you when you need help.&quot; and, &quot;Don't steal your own family's food because they will really not appreciate it.&quot; He does have a role as a Creation Figure, but it is rarely intentional on Anansi's part. Through his actions humans became wise, but it was because Anansi had taken all of the wisdom, shoved it in a pot, and was trying to hide it from humans. He fell off a ladder, it shattered to the world below, and it spread over humanity. He accidentally, sort of, lead Owu, Death Itself, to humanity causing humans to become mortal.<br /> <br /> Anansi isn't regarded as an Abosom, not really. He is not worshiped, he does not reside within an object, location, or animal. He is this... entity who was around at the start of creation. Very powerful, capable of occasionally actually defying the will of Nyame, but not someone you would want around.<br /> <br /> Since I mentioned Nyame a few times there, I'll talk about him next. Nyame is the only God in the Ashanti Pantheon. He is the Supreme Being, and all supernatural power flows through him. He gives it to Abosom, and Suman, and they can impart it to humanity. He was once very close with humanity, literally floating above their shoulders. But an old woman grinding food kept knocking Nyame in the head while doing this, and he eventually withdrew to Osoro, the Heavens. He is benevolent, and cares about humanity a great deal. He has blessed the Royal Family of England, he sent the Golden Chair to the Ashanti, and is generally a really good guy. He is <i>incredibly</i> distant however, and All Powerful, Omnipotent and borderline Omniscentient. (The only challenge to saying he is truly Omniscentient is some of Anansi's tricks) The only real issue Nyame has is when he created Owu, Death, he made himself mortal. Owu isn't evil though, and relies on Nyame for power, so things get along rather well.<br /> <br /> Nyame was a big challenge from our perspectives since he is this huge Supreme God, and the only true God of the Pantheon in accordance to Ashanti belief. We made sure he was not the result of Abrahamic Faiths since &quot;Omnipotent, Benevolent Supreme Sky God,&quot; rings a few bells, but there is evidence of his veneration going back far enough that he is clearly not the result of Christianization efforts, and he doesn't appear to be the result of far earlier Islamic contact. He is a really fun entity though, the Ashanti cosmology pretty much entirely benevolent due to him, short of Anansi, but he isn't really antagonistic. Witches do show up, but they're a whole 'nother sort of thing.<br /> <br /> Asase Ya is a counterpart to Nyame, where he is the Sky, she is the Earth. Similar to Nyame, she is directly worshiped, which is not ordinary. She has no priests, no temples, just some shrines with herself and Nyame sharing the basic conceptual idea of these shrines. She is not an Abosom, and she is not a Goddess. But she is very powerful, very old, and worshiped. So, what can you do. She is prayed to for protection in life, and in death, since mortals enter into her body when they die. She is the creator of the Underworld, which is interesting since Nyame also is said to have created everything. Has some farming associations and taboos based on breaking soil on Thursday since it is her sacred day.<br /> <br /> Lastly, for these 'Big Figures,' there is Tano. He is the greatest of the Abosom (keep in mind that Nyame, Asase Ya, and Anansi are not Abosom. Nyame is above them, Asase Ya is noted to not be a part, and Anansi just doesn't fit into any category) and is the Patron Deity of the Ashanti people. One of Nyame's sons, he is the Abosom of the Tano river, and serves as a guide to his people. We even have records of him speaking to a British Anthropologist through one of his priests at his main locus of worship. He is rather helpful, The King Within The Rock (he resides in a rock in a cave near the start of the river) and has protected humanity in several ways. The biggest is when he made a deal with Owu which resulted in only some humans who got sick dying, rather than all of them.<br /> <br /> Interestingly, Tano is really misogynistic. He has a whole bunch of anti-woman taboos, and is generally regarded as not liking women when he is possessing one of his priests. This is rather out of the ordinary, and he doesn't allow his priests to ever be women. It is never explained <i>why</i><i>, </i>which makes it a rather interesting story hook I suppose. This is not ordinary at all for the Abosom. There are some taboos that specifically target women, but Tano specifically just does not like women at all.<br /> <br /> The Titan situation is probably going to be somewhat odd for them. They don't really have any antagonistic entities, as I mentioned. Nyame just... wouldn't let that happen, so, no Apep, no Fenrir, no Balor, nothing like that. The best we can come up with are two <i>very powerful</i> and essentially 'one-note' beings. One of the oldest Ashanti prayers, one which is frequently mentioned, mention four beings. Nyame, Asase Ya, and &quot;The Creature That Rules the Underworld,&quot; along with, &quot;The Lord of the Forest,&quot; which are where we found our two Titans. Owu, who is Death (Brer Dead in Jamaican folklore) and hangs out in Samando is as far as anyone can work out The Creature that Rules the Underworld. Osebo, the Leopard (you have probably heard of him called Lion, or Tiger in folklore, Mr. Nancy talks about him a few times in American Gods as Tiger) is the Lord of the Forest.<br /> <br /> Then there are an absolute <i>ton</i> of localized, lesser Deities who could work for Divine Parents as well, but might only be able to muster a fraction of a normal entry. If I was doing them for RAW Scion 1e, I could probably only hand out two Abilities, and like one Purview/Epic for some of the really badly attested ones, simply because we just don't have their myths since they are so localized. There are a handful who could work as normal Lesser Gods though, the <i>Ntoro</i> Deities, sort of like local progenitor figures. Bosommuru, a large Python, or, well, an Abosom residing inside a large Python technically who explained to some mortals how to procreate, becoming their descent group's patron.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 06-05-2017, 06:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-11T13:53:55-07:00","id":1102601,"title":"","content":"Depending on exactly what/how that shakes out to be in the final system, &quot;distant supreme deity&quot; concepts like Nyame might fit as a unusually human-well-being-interested Titan or Primordial (I think was the term)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>You need a picture altered to fit your Exalted character, or just looking for some visual inspiration? Check out the twice-reborn <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/266-photoshop-exaltification\" target=\"_blank\"> Exaltification thread</a>. And here is my <a href=\"http://danelsan.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Deviantart page</a><br /> <br /> S\u00f8ren Kierkegaard + Kim Kardashian = <a href=\"https://twitter.com/KimKierkegaard\" target=\"_blank\">Brilliance</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/868-danelsan","name":"danelsan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar868_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-11T23:02:46-07:00","id":1102712,"title":"","content":"I've mentioned wanting to see the Berber and Nubian pantheons before, though I also mentioned that they're both rather deeply entwined with the Netjer.<br /> <br /> The Egyptians and the Berbers shared several major deities, such as Amun/Amon and Isis, with the Egyptians even saying that some of their gods came from Libya - this, understandably, makes it somewhat difficult to make them their own distinct pantheon. Originally I suggested giving them the same relation to the Netjer that the Loa have with the Yoruba, but now that we know pantheons can share gods this doesn't seem necessary. The sheer scale of the crossover still poses some challenges, but at this point I'd say that simply researching them would be a bigger challenge now.<br /> <br /> As for the Nubian pantheon, well... the Nubians and the Egyptians had a lot of cultural crossover as well, which means that they likely shared a few deities as well. The major problem, however, comes from the 'black pharoah' era when the Nubians took over Egypt - in order to gain legitimacy/good will they swapped over to the worshipping the Netjer, which resulted in a lot of knowledge about the Nubian pantheon being lost. As it is, there are only a handful of Nubian gods that I can find any information about at all, and one of those gods has enough 'data' for me to even begin making a profile for them. So if the Nubian pantheon did appear, it would likely be very small and as some sort of 'sub pantheon' for the Netjer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-16T09:10:09-07:00","id":1104317,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1100618#post1100618\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I can remember Gotham By Night talking about how Anansi was the only real viable figure within the Ashanti religious system, which at first was my concern when looking into their traditional religious system. When Sacerdos and I realized that there were a whole host of Deities, though admittedly most of whom are incredibly minor or localized, it suddenly appeared to be totally viable as a Pantheon. There are four figures who are undoubtedly possible Divine Parents of enough note to be considered 'Major Divinities' (I don't say Deities or Gods here since technically only one of them is a God, and only one of them is an Abosom, it's very technical) which is good enough.<br /> <br /> The first of whom, the one everyone knows, is Anansi. He serves as an object lesson in why you should be a productive member of society. Anansi is lazy, greedy, doesn't help his neighbors, and is constantly trying to challenge the will of Nyame. Anansi in most stories gets his comeuppance at the end of it. Anansi is an object lesson, &quot;Don't ignore your neighbors when they need help because they won't help you when you need help.&quot; and, &quot;Don't steal your own family's food because they will really not appreciate it.&quot; He does have a role as a Creation Figure, but it is rarely intentional on Anansi's part. Through his actions humans became wise, but it was because Anansi had taken all of the wisdom, shoved it in a pot, and was trying to hide it from humans. He fell off a ladder, it shattered to the world below, and it spread over humanity. He accidentally, sort of, lead Owu, Death Itself, to humanity causing humans to become mortal.<br /> <br /> Anansi isn't regarded as an Abosom, not really. He is not worshiped, he does not reside within an object, location, or animal. He is this... entity who was around at the start of creation. Very powerful, capable of occasionally actually defying the will of Nyame, but not someone you would want around.<br /> <br /> Since I mentioned Nyame a few times there, I'll talk about him next. Nyame is the only God in the Ashanti Pantheon. He is the Supreme Being, and all supernatural power flows through him. He gives it to Abosom, and Suman, and they can impart it to humanity. He was once very close with humanity, literally floating above their shoulders. But an old woman grinding food kept knocking Nyame in the head while doing this, and he eventually withdrew to Osoro, the Heavens. He is benevolent, and cares about humanity a great deal. He has blessed the Royal Family of England, he sent the Golden Chair to the Ashanti, and is generally a really good guy. He is <i>incredibly</i> distant however, and All Powerful, Omnipotent and borderline Omniscentient. (The only challenge to saying he is truly Omniscentient is some of Anansi's tricks) The only real issue Nyame has is when he created Owu, Death, he made himself mortal. Owu isn't evil though, and relies on Nyame for power, so things get along rather well.<br /> <br /> Nyame was a big challenge from our perspectives since he is this huge Supreme God, and the only true God of the Pantheon in accordance to Ashanti belief. We made sure he was not the result of Abrahamic Faiths since &quot;Omnipotent, Benevolent Supreme Sky God,&quot; rings a few bells, but there is evidence of his veneration going back far enough that he is clearly not the result of Christianization efforts, and he doesn't appear to be the result of far earlier Islamic contact. He is a really fun entity though, the Ashanti cosmology pretty much entirely benevolent due to him, short of Anansi, but he isn't really antagonistic. Witches do show up, but they're a whole 'nother sort of thing.<br /> <br /> Asase Ya is a counterpart to Nyame, where he is the Sky, she is the Earth. Similar to Nyame, she is directly worshiped, which is not ordinary. She has no priests, no temples, just some shrines with herself and Nyame sharing the basic conceptual idea of these shrines. She is not an Abosom, and she is not a Goddess. But she is very powerful, very old, and worshiped. So, what can you do. She is prayed to for protection in life, and in death, since mortals enter into her body when they die. She is the creator of the Underworld, which is interesting since Nyame also is said to have created everything. Has some farming associations and taboos based on breaking soil on Thursday since it is her sacred day.<br /> <br /> Lastly, for these 'Big Figures,' there is Tano. He is the greatest of the Abosom (keep in mind that Nyame, Asase Ya, and Anansi are not Abosom. Nyame is above them, Asase Ya is noted to not be a part, and Anansi just doesn't fit into any category) and is the Patron Deity of the Ashanti people. One of Nyame's sons, he is the Abosom of the Tano river, and serves as a guide to his people. We even have records of him speaking to a British Anthropologist through one of his priests at his main locus of worship. He is rather helpful, The King Within The Rock (he resides in a rock in a cave near the start of the river) and has protected humanity in several ways. The biggest is when he made a deal with Owu which resulted in only some humans who got sick dying, rather than all of them.<br /> <br /> Interestingly, Tano is really misogynistic. He has a whole bunch of anti-woman taboos, and is generally regarded as not liking women when he is possessing one of his priests. This is rather out of the ordinary, and he doesn't allow his priests to ever be women. It is never explained <i>why</i><i>, </i>which makes it a rather interesting story hook I suppose. This is not ordinary at all for the Abosom. There are some taboos that specifically target women, but Tano specifically just does not like women at all.<br /> <br /> The Titan situation is probably going to be somewhat odd for them. They don't really have any antagonistic entities, as I mentioned. Nyame just... wouldn't let that happen, so, no Apep, no Fenrir, no Balor, nothing like that. The best we can come up with are two <i>very powerful</i> and essentially 'one-note' beings. One of the oldest Ashanti prayers, one which is frequently mentioned, mention four beings. Nyame, Asase Ya, and &quot;The Creature That Rules the Underworld,&quot; along with, &quot;The Lord of the Forest,&quot; which are where we found our two Titans. Owu, who is Death (Brer Dead in Jamaican folklore) and hangs out in Samando is as far as anyone can work out The Creature that Rules the Underworld. Osebo, the Leopard (you have probably heard of him called Lion, or Tiger in folklore, Mr. Nancy talks about him a few times in American Gods as Tiger) is the Lord of the Forest.<br /> <br /> Then there are an absolute <i>ton</i> of localized, lesser Deities who could work for Divine Parents as well, but might only be able to muster a fraction of a normal entry. If I was doing them for RAW Scion 1e, I could probably only hand out two Abilities, and like one Purview/Epic for some of the really badly attested ones, simply because we just don't have their myths since they are so localized. There are a handful who could work as normal Lesser Gods though, the <i>Ntoro</i> Deities, sort of like local progenitor figures. Bosommuru, a large Python, or, well, an Abosom residing inside a large Python technically who explained to some mortals how to procreate, becoming their descent group's patron.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your research is, as always, a genuine pleasure to read.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines.</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/user?u=451515\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Vance's Patreon</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance","name":"Robert Vance","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-16T10:23:39-07:00","id":1104352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Robert Vance</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1104317#post1104317\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your research is, as always, a genuine pleasure to read.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, thank so you much! I am so glad you enjoy reading it! I love genuinely knowing that people have fun reading all of what I put together. It makes it all worth it to know that people like to hear what I have dug up along with Sacerdos!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-06T14:31:24-08:00","id":1156478,"title":"","content":"I'm doing some digging into the Nyanga pantheon - lets call them the Milungu.<br /> <br /> Nyamurairi: God of fire, earthquakes, and volcanos; king of the subterranean realm of the gods<br /> <br /> Mukiti: Serpentine river god<br /> <br /> Muisa: God of the dead<br /> <br /> Kahindo: Goddess of good fortune, daughter of Muisa<br /> <br /> Yana: Bat god, uncle to the culture hero Mwindo, he &amp; his bats are the forge-workers for Nyamurairi<br /> <br /> Kitundukutu: Cricket god and spy-master/collector of all gossip of the gods<br /> <br /> Mweri: Moon goddess<br /> <br /> Kubikubi: God of stars &amp; the sky, in reversal to most pantheons, secondary to the subterranean fire god Nyamurairi, though lord of his own domain<br /> <br /> Kiruka: the rain goddess, and old woman, and mother to Nkuba<br /> <br /> Nkuba: the vengeful lightning god<br /> <br /> Kentse: sun god<br /> <br /> Ongo: god of those yet to be born in his subterranean realm, molds the human form in the womb<br /> <br /> Mwindo: Ascended culture hero<br /> <br /> Mpaca: Possibly a Titan, but could also be a god of nocturnal dangers hostile to mankind","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-06T14:44:46-08:00","id":1156485,"title":"","content":"I'd enjoy seeing the Zulu pantheon. (I'm not sure exactly how separate they are from other Bantu cultures.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-06T15:00:43-08:00","id":1156491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1156485#post1156485\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd enjoy seeing the Zulu pantheon. (I'm not sure exactly how separate they are from other Bantu cultures.)</div> </div> </div> </div>When Sacerdos and I were discussing a non-Western African Pantheon to do, the Zulu came up in conversation. Apparently they would be rather difficult to do due to henotheistic leanings. It would probably be possible though, it would just require developing a methodology for henotheistic systems. Hopefully 2e will work better for this sort of thing, and will make bringing henotheistic Pantheons to life easier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-06T15:03:41-08:00","id":1156492,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1156485#post1156485\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd enjoy seeing the Zulu pantheon. (I'm not sure exactly how separate they are from other Bantu cultures.)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> They're distinct, but I'm having a hard time finding that many deities. Bantu traditions tend to pretty focused on single creator deities + ancestors, which is why I was so surprised to find the Nyanga pantheon. Like Watcher said, most Bantu traditions are heavily henotheistic.<br /> <br /> The pantheon name for the Zulu would be the oNkulunkulu (which is the plural for divinity, with Unkulunkulu or uThixo being the primordial creator).<br /> <br /> Pantheon members include Umvelinqangi as the sky father figure often conflated with Unkulunkulu, but more on what Scion would treat as the &quot;God&quot; level (though interestingly he sometimes precedes Unkulunkulu, creating him from the reeds of Uthlanga), Mamlambo the mother of rivers, and Nomkhubulwane, the agriculture and beer goddess of the rainbow.<br /> <br /> Uhlakanyana could be a deity. He's a malevalent trickster dwarf who likes to challenge humans to fights and contests. Defeat means death, but he hands out magic powers to those who win. Some sources seem treat this as a race though, ala faeries, rather than an individual.<br /> <br /> There's also Uthlanga, a swamp that is also a deity and might be a primordial or titan, that Umvelinqangi wed and bore Unkulunkulu.<br /> <br /> Unwaba is a chameleon who's a divine messenger, but honestly that guy gets all over Africa in different names. Even Yoruban tradition has Olorun use a chameleon as his divine messenger.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 11-06-2017, 06:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-07T06:36:04-08:00","id":1156658,"title":"","content":"@Watcher<br /> <br /> Who do you see as a viable Divine Parent/Patron for the Abosom beside Tano and Anansi? I\u2019m assuming Nyame and Asase Ya are your typical Sky Father and Earth Mother Primordials who many Pantheons claim descent from?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-07T08:19:15-08:00","id":1156687,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1156658#post1156658\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">@Watcher<br /> <br /> Who do you see as a viable Divine Parent/Patron for the Abosom beside Tano and Anansi? I\u2019m assuming Nyame and Asase Ya are your typical Sky Father and Earth Mother Primordials who many Pantheons claim descent from?</div> </div> </div> </div>The four of them would be totally viable Divine Parents or Patrons, but there is also the Ancestors, and the <i>Ntoro </i>Deities who would make viable patrons, and Divine parents.<br /> <br /> Nyame and Asase Ya look like your typical Sky Father and Earth Mother at a glance, but they're actually really-really different. Nyame doesn't really have a super-duper Virile Father, he is just a really benevolent Creator Deity who happens to live in the sky, and control its functions. He isn't actually a personification of the sky a-la Ouranos, it's just his place of residence, and his primary area of control. Essentially, think of it like he created the Abosom to delegate out the functions of reality, but he didn't delegate out the functions of the heavens, and thus manages those himself. Asase Ya on the other hand <i>isn't </i>personified, she isn't anthropomorphised, she just <i>is</i> the earth. She doesn't have any of the Maternalistic angles, instead being the creator of the Underworld (she houses the dead within her body), protector of humanity when both dead and alive, and involved with various taboos about farming. So, at a glance, the pair look very 'Standard Gaia-Ouranos situation,' but they're actually <i>really</i> different. Nyame and Asase Ya are also not a couple, Nyame has a bunch of wives, but she isn't one of them.<br /> <br /> While there is the phrase, &quot;All are the children of Nyame, none are the children of Asase,&quot; this is less about Nyame being an All-Father and instead discussing Nyame's caring, fatherly love for his creations, both Abosom, and humanity. Nyame is the creator of all of the beings in reality (except Anansi, and possibly Asase Ya) not through your 'standard' &quot;had sex with a rock,&quot; or, &quot;ejaculated on the dirt,&quot; sort of creator, but instead very much a physical creation. Nyame doesn't have the virile angle going on, he just 'makes' stuff.<br /> <br /> The other potential Divine Parents and Patrons are the <i>Ntoro</i> Deities, which are... sort of really complicated. Okay, so, if the Ashanti were an Imperial Group, there were sub-clans which were the <i>Ntoro</i> groups, a lot like an Irish tuath, or the Haida Eagle-Raven family groups. You can't have a child with someone who is part of your own <i>Ntoro</i> group for example, and the <i>Ntoro</i> has a whole aspect about the soul, and reincarnation, and the like. The <i>Ntoro</i> Deities are the patron Deities of these various <i>Ntoro</i> groups, providing their mortals with the capacity to procreate, and serving as their patron. Depending on who your <i>Ntoro</i> Deity is, you will have a collection of various taboos. The most well known of these is Bosommuru, a great python that rests beneath a lake. Rather nice fellow. There is a whole host of these <i>Ntoro</i> Deities.<br /> <br /> Then the Ancestors. They would make viable Patrons to a Scion. Ashanti minor and major Kings (rulers of an individual clan, or the entire empire) are invested with the divine force of the clan or empire through special stools. Black Stools which important ancestors linger around for the clan groups, or The Golden Stool for the entire empire itself. These minor and major Kings would be perfect Scions, <i>except</i> they pick up a <i>ton</i> of taboos that would make playing the game really difficult. They could be awesome NPC Scions, or if a player wants to take a really big challenge that would put Geasa to shame, they could give it a bash. These are my general notes on the subject of this challenge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ignoring the fact that a Chieftain or King would have their weeks consumed by ritual practices in specific geographic locations, making adventuring slightly difficult, these taboos cripple the capacity of an Ashanti Chieftain or King to be a viable player character: The King of the Ashanti must never touch the ground, must never stumble, be hit, or hit another. They must not eat in public, must not drink in public, must always be in perfect health, remain physically unblemished, and must go to bed early every night. If these taboos are not kept, the King is divested of their Kingship, their divine nature vanishing, and ceases to be of any significance. Chieftains can similarly have their power ripped from them through a political process where they are removed from their office, for reasons such as failing to perform a ritual, violating one of their taboos, cowardice, and a myriad of other reasons. While these positions within the Ashanti Empire can be viably replicated with the Scion template, due to the fact one can spontaneously </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>cease</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> to be a Scion and due to the many taboos, it would be exceptionally difficult for a player or Band to cope with this. Further, there already is currently a King of the Ashanti who has sat upon the Golden Stool, and there can only be one at a time.</span> </div> </div> </div>The two Titans might also make okay Divine Parents to a player who wants to do something super weird, but, who knows. After the Scions of Titans rules are published, we could go back through all of the Pantheons we have done and see if we could implement the system. There are also just sort of random Abosom who are Divine Parents in folklore. The Abosom of a palm tree is one I believe.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-13T05:11:18-08:00","id":1158137,"title":"","content":"Watcher, would you mind describing a few of the Nitoro deities, and maybe some of those random ones like the Palm Tree Abosom? I like to have a base roster of at least twelve for each major pantheon (that isn't one that's subservient to another pantheon, or only has a few surviving members), and I was wondering, in addition to Nyame, Asase Ya, Tano, and Anansi, if there were eight more or so which you'd consider to be good choices for divine patrons specifically?<br /> <br /> And another question, to anyone: Does anyone know much about the Mbuti and Khoisan pantheons? I'd really like to use those pantheons, giving that they each have a fairly unique niche, and I do have a few members for each, but information has overall been scarce. Does anyone have any more info, or know where I could find it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-13T06:11:17-08:00","id":1158152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1158137#post1158137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Watcher, would you mind describing a few of the Nitoro deities, and maybe some of those random ones like the Palm Tree Abosom? I like to have a base roster of at least twelve for each major pantheon (that isn't one that's subservient to another pantheon, or only has a few surviving members), and I was wondering, in addition to Nyame, Asase Ya, Tano, and Anansi, if there were eight more or so which you'd consider to be good choices for divine patrons specifically?<br /> <br /> And another question, to anyone: Does anyone know much about the Mbuti and Khoisan pantheons? I'd really like to use those pantheons, giving that they each have a fairly unique niche, and I do have a few members for each, but information has overall been scarce. Does anyone have any more info, or know where I could find it?</div> </div> </div> </div>There definitely aren't eight more for the Abosom. We did a fair bit of research on them, and apart from those four, everyone else is just so incredibly local and minor that we couldn't flesh them out <i>at all</i>. A minimum of twelve is very unwieldy for the majority of the world's pantheons, I think, since most pre-modern cultures show only a very small, hard core of important deities with a huge number of localised cults. Of the pantheons we're currently working on, we're seeing 7 major gods for the Manchu pantheon, 10 for the Hittites, 4 for the Ta\u00edno, 8 for the Haida, and, as mentioned, 4 for the Ashanti. The only ones out of these you could somehow construe to have 12 gods at all, including minor ones, would be the Hittites.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-13T06:46:03-08:00","id":1158158,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I'll second what Sacerdos said. There <i>are</i> more than 12 Abosom, but a lot of them are so minor all we know are their names. The Palm Tree Abosom just has a Scion and withdraws from their life in the story, they are not named, or anything like that. They're just the Abosom of a specific palm tree. Couldn't really flesh them out without just totally making things up which we're not comfortable doing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-13T10:05:05-08:00","id":1158205,"title":"","content":"Well, thanks anyways. Just my compulsive need for balanced numbers, which isn't particularly realistic.<br /> <br /> I didn't realize that you were working on a write-up for the Zemis, though. Which four deities are you referring to for them? Any Titans? Regardless, I'm glad you're doing that because while they're really interesting they're not the most well-known pantheon so I'm glad that someone is going to make a write-up for them available.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-13T10:37:05-08:00","id":1158212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1158205#post1158205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, thanks anyways. Just my compulsive need for balanced numbers, which isn't particularly realistic.<br /> <br /> I didn't realize that you were working on a write-up for the Zemis, though. Which four deities are you referring to for them? Any Titans? Regardless, I'm glad you're doing that because while they're really interesting they're not the most well-known pantheon so I'm glad that someone is going to make a write-up for them available.</div> </div> </div> </div>Currently, the main god roster is Yucah\u00fa, Attabeira, Guabancex, and Demin\u00e1n Caracaracol, but don't take my word for it just yet. The Zem\u00ed are an incredible mess, I have considered scrapping them entirely at least five times since I'm started, and I'm still not quite sure what to do with them. They are about as fragmentarily attested as the Gauls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-14T15:12:40-08:00","id":1158656,"title":"","content":"I just discovered the Serer religion. It has a really cool and complex cosmology and a handful of gods. I'm really excited to dive into and see if I can find more resources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11009-starman","name":"StarMan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-27T22:50:49-08:00","id":1163176,"title":"","content":"StarMan, would you be willing to give a basic overview of the main Serer deities or provide a source where I could find out about them? That's a pantheon I didn't know existed so of course I'd like them for the UDL.<br /> <br /> I stumbled upon something odd concerning the Ashanti. In a book entitled The Complete Book of Devils and Demons by Leonard R.N. Ashley, in a list of demons/titans/evil gods, a being called Tando was referenced. He was described as being the primary god of evil of the Ashanti, and demanded human sacrifice, taking seven men and seven women at a time. A Google search brought me to a one-sentence article on Encyclopedia Mythica where it described him as a river god who was married to a malevolent goddess named Katarwiri. In the Guide to the Gods by Marjorie Leach, I looked him up and it confirmed both of those things, adding that he was also a god of storms, and would use disease, lightning, and floods to punish people. Katarwiri also had an entry but it didn't say anything more about her than that she was an evil river goddess that also demanded sacrifices. Watcher and Sacerdos, are you familiar with either of these beings? It might be bullshit, and you would know that way better then I, but that would seem to go against the Ashanti having a purely benevolent cosmology.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-28T00:14:15-08:00","id":1163185,"title":"","content":"Bunyoro gods could exist as a Scion pantheon.<br /> <br /> There's<br /> <br /> Nyamionga, the king of the underworld<br /> <br /> Mulindwa, the goddess of the royal clan<br /> <br /> Muhingo, the god of war<br /> <br /> Wamala, the god of plenty<br /> <br /> Ndhaula, the god of disease<br /> <br /> Or they could also work as a sub-pantheon to the Bantu pantheon under Ruhanga, the Supreme Creator.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Pantheon(s):<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LMQFZLq3NrOHs2cp7OWnfukuHCNI4h_0/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Nat, the Spirits of Myanmar</a><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WDvOmQCR3a25WbVowfe8KgGbGLU0yzX3/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Phi, the Spirits of the Tai People</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13670-svarogg","name":"Svarogg","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13670_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"45"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-28T06:51:59-08:00","id":1163218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1163176#post1163176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I stumbled upon something odd concerning the Ashanti. In a book entitled The Complete Book of Devils and Demons by Leonard R.N. Ashley, in a list of demons/titans/evil gods, a being called Tando was referenced. He was described as being the primary god of evil of the Ashanti, and demanded human sacrifice, taking seven men and seven women at a time. A Google search brought me to a one-sentence article on Encyclopedia Mythica where it described him as a river god who was married to a malevolent goddess named Katarwiri. In the Guide to the Gods by Marjorie Leach, I looked him up and it confirmed both of those things, adding that he was also a god of storms, and would use disease, lightning, and floods to punish people. Katarwiri also had an entry but it didn't say anything more about her than that she was an evil river goddess that also demanded sacrifices. Watcher and Sacerdos, are you familiar with either of these beings? It might be bullshit, and you would know that way better then I, but that would seem to go against the Ashanti having a purely benevolent cosmology.</div> </div> </div> </div>Looks like Leonard R.N. Ashley was an English Professor at Brooklyn College, and wrote a series of Occult dictionaries. If I had one on hand I could give a better commentary of it, but, world-spanning encyclopedias tend not to be super accurate. No one can be an expert of everything, it's not really anyone's fault. It's why Dictionaries of Wold History tend to be mostly for public consumption since they will only be able to hit on the 'main points' and inevitably will make mistakes since you are talking about hundreds of fields of history at once. Similarly for Marjorie Leach who is an Independent Scholar. I'm not sure of the validity of her findings.<br /> <br /> If you want a good test of how valid something like this is, check out Gaulish Deities, and you can see how well researched into obscure areas it is. If Teutates is described as a singular Deity, then they are using an outdated theory which was revised in the 1930s - 1940s (it is a class of Deity, not a singular Deity) and if Cernunnos is described as a God of the Hunt or the Wilderness, it is behind archeological findings by about 30 years. (We can find no significant evidence of subsistence hunting in Gaul during the immediatly pre-Roman and post-Roman period, and the Wilderness thing is just speculative)<br /> <br /> But, I can somewhat explain what I think is going on. Within the Ashanti cosmological system there can't be God of Evil. Nyame is a Benelovent Over-Deity, and all supernatural power in the world has to be something he doles out, or doled out from one of the beings he has given power to. Not in a &quot;I make you the God of Fire, now you are the God of Fire,&quot; sort of way, but think of it like channeling his raw power through a feudal pyramid. This is how Abosom, and mortals all can tap into supernatural powers. Due to this, you can't exactly have an Evil Abosom since Nyame would just not give them any power.<br /> <br /> This idea of Tando as a God of Evil is something that jumps out of me as one of two things. At a glance, it sounds like Christianization. Ashanti religion falls prey to Christianization in the period after we are looking at it, and Nyame becomes forcefully reshaped into YHWH. Colonialism does messed up stuff. I expect that the lack of any antagonistic entities necessitated the creation of ones for the converters since Christianity sort of needs that dualistic opposite for its cosmological system, and that's probably where Tando is coming from.<br /> <br /> The other option is that Tando is a foreign Deity who becomes part of one of the Witch-Cults. These are the only real form of semi-antagonistic Divine entities since they're not actually part of Ashanti cosmology, they are all imports, or foreign-inspired entities detached from the Abosom and traditional belief systems. Veneration and service to these Non-Abosom Deities is a method for people to gain malignant powers and use them for the detriment of a community. They're super-illegal, and the Ashanti priest class probably is not too keen on them. They become more popular in the early 20th century, which is after the period we are snapshotting. They at best don't really surpass Hero level antagonism however, not really the realm of Titans. They are entities that mortals are able to come into conflict with and emerge victorious.<br /> <br /> I quickly checked the Encyclopedia Mythica for the entry on the pair of them, and apparently Katarwiri is also from Tshi, and I suspect this is the source of confusion. The pair of them may be foreign Deities who were slowly being imported into the Ashanti regions after the period we are focusing on. Generally... be careful using Encyclopedia Mythica. They have a Sisyphean task to try to work on that impossible project, and don't list where their information is being drawn from. For example, they might be drawing their understanding of Katarwiri and Tando from Marjorie Leach's work, and thus it is just becoming a bit self-referential. Further, since the project can't have experts on literally every field, people working on the project don't necessarily specialize in the specific area in question. The author of both of those articles, Micha F. Lindemans, is a Classicist. No fault on them, they just don't have the resources to hire specialists on every culture known to humanity.<br /> <br /> Encyclopedia Mythica, for example, lists Balor as a God of Death, and that he killed Nuada with his eye. Balor's not a God, also the Irish don't use the God of X program, and while Balor <i>did</i> kill Nuada, in neither manuscript version of the text does it describe him using his eye for such. The section on Blodeuwedd has several explanations for her actions which are undescribed in any translations I have read. The section on Lugh has the misunderstanding that he is the patron of skills rather than being related to the Men of Art (not artists, it's the high-status professions) and thus a patron of the upper 20% of the population (think of Lugh as the God of a Class System if you want to use the God of X system) and... actually, that Lugh article is terrible. He isn't the patron of Lugodunum, that's Lug. Rosmerta is the consort (sometimes) of Lug, not Lugh. Lugnasad has no known ripening associations, that's fictional. I have zero idea where the Lynx association is from, and his exploits are <i>not</i> told in the <i>T\u00e1in B\u00f3 C\u00faailnge</i>, they are told in <i>Cath Maige Tuired</i>, Lugh functions as an ancillary character in the Tain, showing up in passing only two times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 11-28-2017, 12:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-26T09:58:57-08:00","id":1189618,"title":"","content":"Do you guys think we should have a singular Bantu pantheon with the Milungu at the core and multiple sub-pantheons including the Zulu and Bunyoro, or keep them as separate as possible?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-26T11:35:59-08:00","id":1189636,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1189618#post1189618\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you guys think we should have a singular Bantu pantheon with the Milungu at the core and multiple sub-pantheons including the Zulu and Bunyoro, or keep them as separate as possible?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the big question with them, isn't it? Similar to the Polynesians, the Bantu are technically just a language group, spread over an enormous geographical expanse, but with a lot of cultural and especially religious overlap. Watcher and I actually had a very short stab at the Balubare, the absolutely amazing gods of the Kingdom of Buganda, but quickly adjourned working on them as the larger Bantu issue began to arise. Now, the actual research we have done on the Bantu is minimal so far, but the general plan for working them as a pantheon has shaped up to be similar to the way we'll be treating the Polynesian gods: a core group of commonly-held gods, a number of important local figures, thorough explanations of how this shapes up as seperate pantheons as well, and a common PSP (Mana for the Polynesians, Ntu for the Bantu). However, this is a lot easier to do for the Polynesians, for the simple reason that they are polytheists who have a hard core of gods shared among almost all of the different Polynesian groups; the Bantu, meanwhile, are monotheists, with only one true and high God. Everyone else you might have heard about are, in fact, just very powerful ancestor spirits, and that is kind of the crucial issue with the Bantu: ancestors are enormously important to them, more important than God, actually, and their &quot;pantheons&quot; are normally actually the deified ancestors and lineage founders of the royal family. Thus, when Watcher and I get to doing our Bantu writeup, we will most likely largely conflate the different Bantu &quot;High Gods&quot; into one, and then outline a number of these royal ancestor-pantheons - so far, I have Bakongo, Buganda, Rwanda, Zimbabwe, and the Zulu noted down as candidates. So the solution we'd do is a combination of both, avoiding both the inaccuracies of a single Bantu pantheon, and the frustrating repetitiveness of separate ones. But that's just how we <i>would </i>do it when we <i>get </i>to doing it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> At the moment, though, Watcher and I have a number of other African pantheons on our plate that seem easier and quicker to do right now than the behemoth that would we the Bantu pantheon(s). <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-26T12:15:50-08:00","id":1189654,"title":"","content":"I think that would be a good way to approach both them and the Polynesians.<br /> <br /> Just how many pantheons are you guys actually planning? You seem to mention more and more every time I speak to you. I know the ones you're focusing on in the immediate future (Ashanti, Haida, Hittite, etc.) but if you're okay with it, and there's not some reason you'd be keeping it to yourself, would you be willing to list all the candidates in your long-term plan? Because I would be very interested in knowing. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-26T13:35:18-08:00","id":1189679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1189654#post1189654\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that would be a good way to approach both them and the Polynesians.<br /> <br /> Just how many pantheons are you guys actually planning? You seem to mention more and more every time I speak to you. I know the ones you're focusing on in the immediate future (Ashanti, Haida, Hittite, etc.) but if you're okay with it, and there's not some reason you'd be keeping it to yourself, would you be willing to list all the candidates in your long-term plan? Because I would be very interested in knowing. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it depends - how many do we want to do? All of them, all that are possible to do, and maybe then some. How many are we going to do? Depends on how many we can do, of course. And how many we are actually doing ... well, that depends on what you define as &quot;doing&quot;. Some are in that very rough outline stage, like the Bantu, some are no more than a list of books that says &quot;could be researched one day&quot;, some are already substantial collections of info, and on others, we are already implementing what rules for 2e we already know. What is very important to us right now is to avoid the pantheons that the Developers have already announces for the various installments of 2e. We don't want to offer a free alternative to any of their material too close to its own publication, so as not to impact sales negatively in even the slightest way. In the meantime, we are concentrating on pantheons that are more obscure, adding more to the already substantial variety of mythologies the developers have promised us.<br /> You know that that latter group includes the Abosom (Ashanti), Shiunesh (Hittite), Enduri (Manchu), Sga'na Qeda's (Haida), and Zem\u00ed (Ta\u00edno) by now. Furthermore, there is a second African pantheon we're doing right now, but since we've spoiled so much of our work already, I think it'd be fun to keep you guys guessing with this one. I'll tell you as much: It has very many tales and very few gods, and all of them are pretty much psychopaths - all of them can be expected to kill people completely at random, without any reason. While their mortals have a sharp division between right and wrong, the gods do not, at all.<br /> So that's what I'd give you - I hope it keeps you interested <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-26T14:24:52-08:00","id":1189693,"title":"","content":"That's certainly awesome. I'm glad to see you focusing on obscure pantheons that the developers aren't likely to choose, the obscure mythologies are certainly fascinating and I'm glad to see different ones rather than double-ups of the official ones.<br /> <br /> I can't say I have any idea which African pantheon it is you're describing, but they sound like a complete counterpoint to the Abosom, being a small African pantheon with a completely malicious roster rather than a benign one. I take it they probably don't get along.<br /> <br /> I definitely find your dedication to researching and constructing them admirable, and I can't wait to see the first ones you release.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-28T17:00:20-08:00","id":1190486,"title":"","content":"Could you give me one more hint on the mystery pantheon: Is it one of the pantheons I have listed in the UDL? I'm just curious if it's one I have any passing knowledge of at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-01T00:15:01-08:00","id":1190583,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1190486#post1190486\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Could you give me one more hint on the mystery pantheon: Is it one of the pantheons I have listed in the UDL? I'm just curious if it's one I have any passing knowledge of at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Haha, I see I've definitely caught your interest. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It is not on your list itself, but a closely related pantheon is. And it is definitely a pantheon you know of. And now I'm probably telling too much already. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-02T14:51:38-08:00","id":1191135,"title":"","content":"I have a guess now which pantheon it is, but I'm not going to say which so not to spoil it for everyone else. The pantheon I'm thinking of though is another one whose most famous member is an animal-themed trickster, and which I mistakenly thought was purely monotheistic while constructing the UDL.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-02T23:01:01-08:00","id":1191261,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1191135#post1191135\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have a guess now which pantheon it is, but I'm not going to say which so not to spoil it for everyone else. The pantheon I'm thinking of though is another one whose most famous member is an animal-themed trickster, and which I mistakenly thought was purely monotheistic while constructing the UDL.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, see, I knew you'd figure it out. I gave away too much <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Though you're probably wrong about the animal the Trickster is about; it's a common misconception, which we are going to clear up <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-03T11:22:07-08:00","id":1191400,"title":"","content":"Cool. I'm very happy to see that particular pantheon. It'll be nice to have a truly nasty pantheon from Africa, given that the Abosom and the Orishas are both overall pretty benevolent. Plus, the character concept I've been developing for myself, if I ever miraculously get the opportunity to be a player for this game, is a scion of Xhuuya who was going to have a vicious rivalry with what I originally pictured as an anti-villainous scion of Iktomi, but a scion of you-know-who might work just as well for the rival.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-27T19:02:51-07:00","id":1200305,"title":"","content":"Man, I'd love to see more African Pantheons. If you could share more that would be great. Especially with the Ashanti.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/717-monghani","name":"Monghani","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar717_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-27T20:41:27-07:00","id":1200330,"title":"","content":"The Abosom (Ashanti) have the highest profile single figure going for them.<br /> <br /> The Milungu (Nyanga) have the potential breadth to stand on par with the detailed pantheons in terms of numbers of major figures.<br /> <br /> So those are definitely the top two picks I think.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-28T03:56:04-07:00","id":1200383,"title":"","content":"I'd also dig seeing something done with the Serer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/717-monghani","name":"Monghani","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar717_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-28T06:47:57-07:00","id":1200409,"title":"","content":"Can anyone tell me anything about the Serer? Wikipedia just lists several, seemingly synonymous, creator deities, which is odd.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-28T12:37:00-07:00","id":1200520,"title":"","content":"Wikipedia lists a Creator God, a Justice/Vengeance God, and a Guardian Sea Goddess to name a few. But their religious and cultural practices are pretty amazing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/717-monghani","name":"Monghani","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar717_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-08T13:11:51-07:00","id":1204662,"title":"","content":"What do people know about the Mbuti pantheon? All I can really find for them is a wikipedia stub, but they sound interesting. Khonvoum in particular seems cool, and they have a couple of cool Titans. Has anyone looked into them as a potential pantheon? If so, does anyone have an idea of who their principle members would be?<br /> <br /> Also, on an unrelated note, which is the correct name, Cagn or Kaang?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","user":"Wannabe Demon Lord","date":". 04-08-2018, 05:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-08T22:20:05-07:00","id":1204793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1204662#post1204662\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do people know about the Mbuti pantheon? All I can really find for them is a wikipedia stub, but they sound interesting. Khonvoum in particular seems cool, and they have a couple of cool Titans. Has anyone looked into them as a potential pantheon? If so, does anyone have an idea of who their principle members would be?<br /> <br /> Also, on an unrelated note, which is the correct name, Cagn or Kaang?</div> </div> </div> </div>\u01c0Kaggen is the more correct form, noted down by a proper linguist. Cagn is basically write-it-as-you-hear-it, courtesy of a certain colonial official. As they were noted among different tribes, though, they are often used parallelly. 'Kaang, meanwhile, is a sky god recorded by a French missionary, which may or may not be related to \u01c0Kaggen.<br /> <br /> Regarding the Mbuti, I've been searching for ages for anything substantial on them, and still haven't found it. Sorry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-01-11T13:14:23-08:00","id":1280165,"title":"","content":"Another question for Watcher and Sacerdos here. I know the Abosom have only four &quot;major&quot; figures, but given that you guys have specific sections in your write-ups for minor deities, are you planning on including any minor gods for them, maybe some of the Ntoro deities? You mentioned \u200bBosommuru by name, is he getting an entry?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-01-11T13:31:38-08:00","id":1280170,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1280165#post1280165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another question for Watcher and Sacerdos here. I know the Abosom have only four &quot;major&quot; figures, but given that you guys have specific sections in your write-ups for minor deities, are you planning on including any minor gods for them, maybe some of the Ntoro deities? You mentioned \u200bBosommuru by name, is he getting an entry?</div> </div> </div> </div>Funny you should ask that, I was just today thinking I should do a Abosom spoiler again these days <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> A lot has changed since we made that statement. The finalised version which will enter editing sometime this year includes <b>five</b> figures, Anansi, Nyame, Asase Ya, Tano, and Akonnedi, as Major Gods, and the god Tutu as well as the Nananom (local guardian deities) and Ntoro deities as templates for Minor Gods. Also, I am by now <i>very</i> unhappy with our work on the San Pantheon so far, so I'm not sure anymore if and how soon they will happen, especially since Watcher is currently all but out of commission for his many other responsibilities. Sorry for that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-01-11T14:42:02-08:00","id":1280182,"title":"","content":"After a very quick Google search, Akonnedi seems to be a mother goddess and a divine arbiter of conflicts. Is this accurate?<br /> Tutu, on the other hand, doesn't really come up with much on Google, largely due to the word having other definitions, I assume. What are they like?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-01-12T09:59:53-08:00","id":1280336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1100228-other-african-pantheons?p=1280182#post1280182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">After a very quick Google search, Akonnedi seems to be a mother goddess and a divine arbiter of conflicts. Is this accurate?<br /> Tutu, on the other hand, doesn't really come up with much on Google, largely due to the word having other definitions, I assume. What are they like?</div> </div> </div> </div>Akonnedi is weird and I'll leave it to your reading of my full write-up of her in a year or so to explain her fully. Suffice to say, she doesn't fit in anywhere. She is a non-Akan goddess adapted into Akan-Ashanti religion, yet still speaking in a foreign language through her oracle. She is a goddess of liberation from social norms in a rigidly stratified slaving society. She is weird. She also helped inaugurate the first President of Ghana. Tutu is a war fetish turned god, a blood-thirsty deity of fighting, fertility, and booze, and kind of another template for a whole category of such deities of which he is only the most prominent. Like Akonnedi, he is also kinda a foreigner.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}